      "content": "I completely understand the need to avoid discriminatory assumptions; that's a core part of our compliance framework. I'm not looking for investment advice, but rather the theoretical framework for how these economic transitions are modeled in urban economics.\n\nLet's look at your suggestion of using \"retail mix shifts\" and \"educational attainment\" as metrics. In a predictive model, how would you technically differentiate between a neighborhood undergoing \"gentrification-led growth\"—where you see a rapid influx of high-income, high-education cohorts—and a neighborhood experiencing \"organic revitalization,\" where growth is more distributed? \n\nSpecifically, what kind of feature interactions between retail density and demographic shifts would a model use to signal that a neighborhood is successfully transitioning into a \"high-stability\" zone? I'm trying to understand the statistical weight that 'premium service' density usually carries in these types of transition models.",
